@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration
@cindex configuration

Codebud has no configuration file of its own.  Runtime behaviour is
controlled through environment variables and through three JSON policy
files.  This chapter documents both, and where Codebud keeps its files.

@menu
* Environment Variables Overview::  Variables that control Codebud.
* LLM Backend Variables::           Choosing Ollama or OpenAI.
* Feature Flags::                   Enabling optional features.
* Policy JSON Files::               The three safety configuration files.
* Harmless Commands::               Commands always allowed.
* Powerful Commands::               Commands allowed with care.
* Trusted Sequences::               Named multi-step sequences.
* Checking Your Configuration::     Using codebud config.
* Files and Directories::           Where data and policy files live.
@end menu


@node Environment Variables Overview
@section Environment Variables Overview
@cindex environment variables

The table below lists every variable Codebud reads, its default, and what
it does.

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .25 .50
@headitem Variable @tab Default @tab Purpose
@item @env{OLLAMA_MODEL}
@tab @emph{(none)}
@tab Model name exactly as it appears in @command{ollama list}.
Required for the Ollama backend.  Can be overridden per invocation
without touching your shell profile --- see @option{--model}/@option{-m}
and @code{/model} in the Usage chapter (@pxref{The run Subcommand}).
@item @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL}
@tab @code{http://localhost:11434}
@tab Base URL of the Ollama REST API.
@item @env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX}
@tab @code{8192}
@tab Context window, in tokens, that Codebud requests from Ollama on
every call.  Ollama's own default (4096) is small enough that long
prompts lose their beginning, which holds the planner's instructions.
The same value is sent on every request, because a change makes Ollama
reload the model and discard its cached prompt prefix.  Larger values use
more memory (roughly 36 KB per token for a 3B Qwen2.5 model).  Invalid
values are ignored with a warning.
@item @env{EMBED_MODEL}
@tab @code{nomic-embed-text}
@tab Dedicated embedding model used by the @code{embed} and
@code{search_embeddings} tools.  It is independent of @env{OLLAMA_MODEL};
a text-generation model makes poor embeddings.  Pull it with
@command{ollama pull nomic-embed-text}.
@item @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}
@tab @emph{(none)}
@tab When set, enables the OpenAI fallback backend if Ollama is
unavailable (requires @code{codebud[openai]}).
@item @env{OPENAI_MODEL}
@tab @code{gpt-3.5-turbo}
@tab OpenAI model to use when the OpenAI backend is active.
@item @env{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED}
@tab @code{0}
@tab Set to @code{1} to enable the @code{web_search} tool.  When
@code{0}, the tool reports itself as unavailable to the planner.
@item @env{XDG_DATA_HOME}
@tab @file{~/.local/share}
@tab Codebud keeps its runtime data in @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud}
(@pxref{Files and Directories}).
@item @env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}
@tab @file{~/.config}
@tab Your own policy files go in @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud}
(@pxref{Policy JSON Files}).
@item @env{CODEBUD_LEGACY_DATA_DIR}
@tab @emph{(the checkout's @file{data/})}
@tab Where versions before October 2026 kept runtime data; copied once into
the data directory.  Rarely needed (the test suite sets it).
@end multitable

To see the resolved value of every variable, run:

@example
codebud config
@end example

To make a variable permanent, add it to your shell profile:

@example
echo 'export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M' >> ~/.zshrc
@end example


@node LLM Backend Variables
@section LLM Backend Variables
@cindex LLM backend
@cindex Ollama backend
@cindex OpenAI backend

Codebud supports two LLM backends, selected by priority:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Ollama} (preferred) --- used when @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} is set and
the Ollama server at @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} is reachable.
Codebud streams tokens using @code{stream=True} so that the HTTP
connection never times out during the long prefill phase on CPU-only
hardware.

@item
@strong{OpenAI} (fallback) --- used when @env{OPENAI_API_KEY} is set and
Ollama is not configured or not reachable.  Needs the optional client:
@code{pip install "codebud[openai]"}; without it Codebud logs a warning
saying so.  The OpenAI call is
non-streaming and uses @code{temperature=0} for deterministic output.
@end enumerate

If neither backend is configured, @command{codebud} logs a warning; the
planner gets no plan, and Codebud falls back to a plain chat reply, which
is then empty (@pxref{No Plan or an Empty Reply}).

@subsection Pointing at a remote Ollama instance

If you run Ollama on a separate machine (for example, a desktop with more
RAM), change @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL}:

@example
export OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.50:11434
export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M
codebud run "your task"
@end example

Codebud does not require the model to be on the same machine as the CLI.

@cindex proxy
@cindex HTTP_PROXY
@subsection HTTP proxies

When @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} points at the local machine (@code{localhost},
@code{127.0.0.1} or @code{::1}), Codebud always connects directly and
ignores @env{HTTP_PROXY}, @env{HTTPS_PROXY} and @env{ALL_PROXY}.  A
system-wide proxy therefore never gets in the way of a local Ollama, even
without a @env{NO_PROXY} setting.

For a remote Ollama the usual proxy variables apply.  If the remote host
must be reached directly, list it in @env{NO_PROXY}:

@example
export NO_PROXY=192.168.1.50
@end example


@node Feature Flags
@section Feature Flags
@cindex feature flags
@cindex web search, enabling

@table @env
@item WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED
Default: @code{0} (disabled).  Set to @code{1} (or @code{true} or
@code{yes}) to enable the @code{web_search} tool.  When enabled, the tool
sends a POST request to @code{https://lite.duckduckgo.com/lite/} and
parses the HTML response.  No API key is required.

@example
WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=1 codebud run "find the latest Python release notes"
@end example

When disabled, the @code{web_search} tool's description in the planner
prompt is replaced with a notice so the planner knows not to use it.
@end table


@node Policy JSON Files
@section Policy JSON Files
@cindex policy files

Three JSON files make up the safety policy that the @code{SafetyEngine}
loads each time @code{AgentCore} starts:

@table @file
@item harmless_commands.json
Commands that only read or report.  @xref{Harmless Commands}.

@item powerful_commands.json
Commands that run code or change files.  They are permitted, but in
@code{auto} mode and in @command{codebud run} (unless @option{--yes}) each
one needs an explicit confirmation.  @xref{Powerful Commands}.

@item trusted_sequences.json
Named multi-step sequences that the planner may propose as a unit.
@xref{Trusted Sequences}.
@end table

Each file is looked up separately, and the first one found wins:

@enumerate
@item
@file{.codebud/@var{name}} in the current project: the nearest
@file{.codebud/} directory at or above the directory Codebud is started
from, found the way Git finds @file{.git/}.
@item
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud/@var{name}} (by default
@file{~/.config/codebud/@var{name}}): your own policy for all projects.
@item
The defaults shipped with Codebud.
@end enumerate

To change the policy, copy a default file to @file{~/.config/codebud/} (or
a project's @file{.codebud/}) and edit the copy; do not edit the shipped
defaults.  @command{codebud config} shows which file is in effect for each
name (@pxref{Checking Your Configuration}).

All three files must be valid JSON arrays.  If a file is malformed, the
@code{SafetyEngine} treats that list as empty and logs a warning;
@command{codebud doctor} validates the files in effect.


@node Harmless Commands
@section Harmless Commands
@cindex harmless_commands.json

@file{harmless_commands.json} lists commands that only read or
report.  A @code{command} step is accepted when its leading words match an
entry exactly (@pxref{The Safety Engine}): @code{"ls"} allows
@code{ls -la} but not @code{lsblk}, and @code{"git log"} allows
@code{git log -n 3} but not @code{git push}.

Default content:

@example
[
  "echo", "cat", "head", "tail", "ls", "pwd", "whoami", "wc",
  "find", "diff", "git log", "git status", "git diff", "git show"
]
@end example

Some arguments are refused even for listed programs, for example
@code{find -exec} and @code{find -delete} (@pxref{The Safety Engine}).

@strong{Note:} be specific with multi-word tools.  Adding @code{"git"}
would permit @emph{all} git subcommands, including @command{git push} and
@command{git reset --hard}; list @code{"git log"}, @code{"git status"} and
so on separately instead.  Do not put programs that run code
(interpreters, test runners, build tools) here; they belong in the
powerful list.


@node Powerful Commands
@section Powerful Commands
@cindex powerful_commands.json

@file{powerful_commands.json} lists commands that run code or
change files.  They are permitted, but they are not trusted blindly: in
@code{auto} mode, where a plan otherwise runs after one approval, Codebud
stops before each powerful step and asks @samp{Run it? [y/N]}
(@pxref{Agent Modes}).  @command{codebud run} asks the same unless it is
given @option{--yes}.  In @code{ask} mode every step is confirmed anyway;
@code{full-auto} runs them without asking.

Default content:

@example
[
  "grep", "sed", "awk", "cp", "mv",
  "python", "python3", "pytest", "ruff"
]
@end example

@code{python}, @code{python3} and @code{pytest} execute arbitrary code (a
script, a test suite's @file{conftest.py}); @code{ruff} rewrites files with
@option{--fix} or @command{ruff format}.  Inline code (@code{python -c}) is
refused outright.

Common additions for a developer workflow:

@example
[
  "grep", "sed", "awk", "cp", "mv",
  "python", "python3", "pytest", "ruff",
  "mkdir", "touch", "chmod",
  "pip install", "npm install", "git add", "git commit"
]
@end example

@strong{Warning:} do not add @code{"rm"}, @code{"sudo"}, or
@code{"shutdown"} to either list.  The sandbox has a separate hard-coded
block for the most destructive patterns (@pxref{The Sandbox}).


@node Trusted Sequences
@section Trusted Sequences
@cindex trusted_sequences.json

@file{trusted_sequences.json} is a JSON array of named
multi-step sequences.  Each entry has a @code{name} and a @code{sequence}
of command prefixes:

@example
[
  @{
    "name": "install_python_package",
    "sequence": ["python3 -m pip install"]
  @}
]
@end example

The @code{SafetyEngine} loads this file but the current implementation does
not apply sequence-level validation beyond what the step-by-step check
already covers.  The file is reserved for future expansion --- for example,
requiring that @command{pip install} is always followed by a test run
before any commit.


@node Checking Your Configuration
@section Checking Your Configuration
@cindex codebud config
@cindex codebud doctor

Two subcommands help verify your configuration:

@table @command
@item codebud config
Prints the resolved value of Codebud's environment variables, including
defaults (except @env{OPENAI_MODEL}), followed by the data directory and
the policy file in effect for each name.  Use this to confirm that your
exported variables are being read correctly.

@example
codebud config
@end example

Example output:

@example
Codebud resolved configuration:
  OLLAMA_MODEL       = qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
  OLLAMA_BASE_URL    = http://localhost:11434
  EMBED_MODEL        = nomic-embed-text
  CODEBUD_NUM_CTX    = 8192
  OPENAI_API_KEY     = (not set)
  WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED = 0

Files:
  data directory           = /home/you/.local/share/codebud
  harmless_commands.json   = /home/you/.config/codebud/harmless_commands.json
  powerful_commands.json   = /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/codebud/data/policy/powerful_commands.json
  trusted_sequences.json   = /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/codebud/data/policy/trusted_sequences.json
@end example

@item codebud doctor
Checks each configuration value for correctness: whether the Ollama server
is reachable, whether the selected model has been pulled, and whether the
three policy JSON files parse without errors.  Each check prints
@samp{[PASS]} or @samp{[FAIL]} with a one-line fix command; optional
items, such as a missing embedding model, print @samp{[WARN]} and do not
count as failures.

@example
codebud doctor
@end example
@end table


@node Files and Directories
@section Files and Directories
@cindex XDG directories
@cindex data directory
@cindex .codebud directory

Codebud's locations do not depend on the directory it is started from.

@multitable @columnfractions .38 .62
@headitem Location @tab Contents
@item @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/} (default @file{~/.local/share/codebud/})
@tab Runtime data: @file{memory/entries.jsonl} (execution history),
@file{sessions/} (chat sessions), @file{embeddings/index.json}.
@item @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud/} (default @file{~/.config/codebud/})
@tab Your policy files, overriding the shipped defaults.
@item @file{.codebud/} in a project
@tab Policy files for that project, overriding both
(@pxref{Policy JSON Files}).
@end multitable

Versions before October 2026 kept runtime data in a @file{data/} directory
inside the source checkout.  On first start, Codebud copies anything found
there into the data directory and reports how many files it copied; the
originals are left in place, and you can delete @file{data/} once you have
checked the new location.

